How to make this resume stronger

For your experience level, the most important point is to position yourself as a Systems Engineer who already understands the technical side of security and is moving into a dedicated security engineering or information system security role—rather than presenting yourself as an entry-level cybersecurity candidate.

I recommend adding specific tools and accomplishments wherever you have hands-on experience.

    Security tools: ACAS/Nessus, Tenable, SCAP, STIG Viewer, Splunk, vulnerability scanners, or SIEM tools.

    ATO/RMF: System Security Plans (SSPs), Security Assessment Reports (SARs), Plans of Action and Milestones (POA&Ms), control implementation evidence, and continuous monitoring.

    DevOps: Jenkins, GitLab CI/CD, Azure DevOps, Git, Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform, Ansible, or PowerShell.

    Security environments: DISA STIGs, NIST SP 800-53, NIST RMF, FISMA, and federal or DoD systems, if applicable.

Only retain tools, standards, and responsibilities that match your actual experience.
Three quick details that would help me tailor this into a stronger, final resume

    What environment have you worked in? Federal/DoD, commercial enterprise, cloud, or a combination?

    Which tools have you used? For example, ACAS/Nessus, Splunk, STIGs, Linux, Windows Server, Jenkins, GitLab, AWS, or Azure.

    Which security role are you targeting? ISSE/Information System Security Engineer, Cybersecurity Engineer, DevSecOps Engineer, or ISSO/RMF/ATO Specialist?

